#{[17592186341852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341852 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341852 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341852 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=y+1 \\\\ x-y=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae55f4eb-c009-586b-a5eb-f77f0f124cd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341852 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=1 \\\\ x-y=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341931 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef354870-3599-55d1-9c3b-8b4fa57a2d1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341931 #edb/ref 1074 "- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341988 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}-1}?$    \n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[0,1)$\n- $[-2,-1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96d14ec6-ae06-5360-8ddd-d401dbb48508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341988 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0$ (it makes zero the denominator).   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval  $[0,1)$   is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,-1]$    is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,-1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341990 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-1,1]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n\nIII. $[-1,0)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b9b884e-eb8a-59c7-aa31-49f0025fb47f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341990 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341991 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[-1,2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1eaad6e-884d-57bd-a4ae-a690d3d6abe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341991 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342003 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-2,2]$\n- $[-3,1)$\n- $[1.5,2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8abc4b9f-caf8-5c94-91da-62ad0ae61b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342003 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342016 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b4e1216-e41e-5317-bf20-2acb3db54b81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342016 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342022 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3624bdf6-d1bf-5873-8a2a-1542d72f9250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342022 #edb/ref 1074 "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z=3 \\\\ y+5z=-8 \\\\ z=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+5(4)=-8\\,⟹\\,y=-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-28$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(-28)-2(4)=3\\,⟹\\,x=39.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=39$, $y=-28$, and $z=4.$ Or, in vector form $⟨39,-28,4⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342029 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16a72be1-2bd1-5d5b-86b7-6ea0a4390bbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342029 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342079 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342079 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-10y=-5 \\\\ 2x-4y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0b4636b-3357-5697-b846-0e6a1da3fd28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342079 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=4$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=4,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342104 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 7 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "061b9ba7-cf18-55a3-aa89-28e4ebe56a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342104 #edb/ref 1074 "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+7z=-9 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-2y+7z=-9\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=-9+2y-7z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-9+2y-7z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9+2y-7z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342116 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342116 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342116 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342116 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$\n." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342116 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0008ebd1-cf28-5d38-a9cd-5c6dde0f95a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342116 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the second and the third  equations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1} & =-16\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{1}=-2$ into the third equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+{x}_{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =3 \\\\ (-2)+2(3) & =3 \\\\ 4 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a false statement. \n\nSo, the system has no solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342117 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342117 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342117 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eec4fe83-c254-5d51-9484-b88391578898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342117 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the first and the second equation we get\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{2}=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =-7 \\\\ {x}_{1}-(4) & =-7 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-7+4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =6 \\\\ 2(-3)+3(4) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+4=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342121 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342121 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342121 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -12 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -19 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342121 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba6ce214-f348-57ed-8c0e-e75dd14f4628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342121 #edb/ref 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -12 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -19 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -19 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y-5z=6 \\\\ 0x+y+3z=-4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-9,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342126 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342126 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342126 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342126 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33debd45-f90c-578d-9c6b-11c54937ac6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342126 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-5 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=-10 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2.5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2.5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-5 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2.5\\,{x}_{3}=8 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2.5(-2)=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=8-5=3.\n$$\n\nFinally,  substituting ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=-2$  into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+2(-2)=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=-2,$ which means \n\n$$\n2{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+3-2=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342195 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342195 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix \n$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is upper triangular\n- $D$ is lower triangular\n- ${D}^{T}$ is upper triangular" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10915988-162b-5dec-9de2-5241d5fc3202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342195 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $D$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. All the entries above the main diagonal of $D$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of ${D}^{T}$ are zeros:\n\n$$\n{D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342208 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342208 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342208 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0e530ca-0510-53a9-a606-7cbe24765900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342208 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/eb/eb1176b4a5e2565fceb03e9af652a6ae875f5ee678c103405045fcebc36342e1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x+2$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+3 & =1(x+1) \\\\ y+3 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+2 \\\\ y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2 & =x-2 \\\\ -x-x & =-2-2 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,-3)$ and $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2$ is $3\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342210 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342210 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/67/67d09cf1ebb51a37c122ea1e0bb53b0bcabedca0251ae4105ebb93dd55d111dd.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7a18e4a-49f2-563e-8c4d-e70b3f9189cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342210 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ and passes through the point $(-4,-1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-8 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =8-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-5.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=-5.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,-5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1)$ is $(2,-5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342212 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342212 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342212 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342212 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342212 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7220b7e3-9cce-5102-874c-014511b9e7f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342212 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342230 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cef0a842-c20f-55db-9989-525f1aca72a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342230 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\nk=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342267 #edb/ref 1080 "-68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342267 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342267 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0205c844-2ff5-5390-b203-852d23c56776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342316 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342316 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342316 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0965e63c-f7d0-50df-a452-4c16065920da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342406 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342406 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342406 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13280805-29d2-5042-b55a-faeede02c8f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342409 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342409 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342409 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13988406-f6f9-5745-b13b-53508dc55bba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342437 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342437 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342437 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "165d7ee9-7041-5c05-bf00-1f870f0ce2ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342445 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342445 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342445 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1771b03e-a8cd-5b43-9af6-33839f2003d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342495 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342495 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592